{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1041,1532p' /Users/iv/.claude/skills/project-management/SKILL.md\"","aggregated_output":"\n---\n\n## Setup & Deployment\n\nTwo deployment modes. Choose based on your use case:\n\n| | Local | Service |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Board storage** | `.task-board/` directory in project | board-server (bbolt + MinIO) |\n| **Multi-agent** | Single agent at a time | Multiple agents concurrently |\n| **Persistence** | Git (committed with project) | Server-side (backed up separately) |\n| **Network** | None | HTTP (LAN or remote) |\n| **Setup time** | Zero — just use CLI | 2 min (make) or 5 min (Docker) |\n| **Best for** | Solo dev, small projects | Teams, swarm agents, persistent boards |\n\n### Local mode (default)\n\nNo board bootstrap needed. CLI creates `.task-board/` on first `create` command.\nIf you run setup/install scripts, profile configs are created automatically:\n\n```bash\n# Canonical workstation setup for CLI/TUI + installed skill artifact\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode local --board-dir .task-board\n\n# Start using\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=my-feature, description=\"...\")'\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\n```\n\nBoard lives in `.task-board/` inside your project. Commit it to git for persistence.\n\nIf this board lives inside a real code repository that agents will work on, bootstrap it from the `skill-project-management` source checkout with repo-local agents enabled:\n\n```bash\ncd /path/to/skill-project-management\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode local --board-dir .task-board --bootstrap-local-agents\n```\n\nThis is the expected companion step for board setup in a project repo:\n- `.agents/` gets the local runtime contents and templates\n- `.claude/` and `.codex/` are refreshed as thin links into `.agents/`\n- spawned agents can reliably see repo-local instructions instead of only global installs\n- agents can call `task-board q 'project_config()'` before work to verify whether this repo uses a local board or a remote profile\n\n### Service mode (board-server)\n\n#### Quick start (local binary)\n\n```bash\n# Install CLI/TUI + project profile via the canonical setup path\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\n\n# If this board drives work in a code repo, prefer the single setup command with repo-local agents\n./scripts/setup.sh --project-dir /path/to/project --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-board my-project --bootstrap-local-agents\n\n# Build board-server binary (binary-only helper)\nmake install-backend\n\n# Start server locally\nboard-server --addr :8080 --data-dir ./data/boards --auth-db ./data/auth.db\n```\n\nServer prints everything you need on startup:\n```\n  board-server running:\n  URL:         http://localhost:8080\n  Data:        ./data/boards\n\n  FIRST RUN — admin token (save it, shown once):\n  tb_xxxxx\n\n  Quick start:\n    export TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\n    export TASK_BOARD_ID=<board-id>\n```\n\nSelect a user, store the token in Keychain, then go:\n```bash\nexport TASK_BOARD_REMOTE=http://localhost:8080\nexport TASK_BOARD_USER=sampleuser\n\n# Create a board\ntask-board admin board create my-project \"My Project\"\n\nexport TASK_BOARD_ID=my-project\ntask-board login sampleuser tb_xxxxx\n\n# Use normally — all DSL commands route to server\ntask-board q --format compact 'summary()'\ntask-board m 'create(type=epic, name=auth-system)'\n\n# Optional: write remote profile config explicitly\n./scripts/setup.sh --mode remote --remote-url http://localhost:8080 --remote-user sampleuser --remote-board my-project\n```\n\n#### Docker deployment (production)\n\nFull stack: Caddy (TLS reverse proxy) + board-server + MinIO (resource storage).\n\n```bash\ncd tools/board-server/deploy\ncp .env.example .env\n# Edit .env: set BOARD_DOMAIN, MinIO credentials\n\n./deploy.sh         # Build + start (prints admin token on first run)\n./status.sh         # Check health\n./logs.sh           # Tail logs\n./backup.sh         # Backup data\n./stop.sh           # Stop all\n./e2e-test.sh       # End-to-end test\n```\n\nFor self-signed TLS (localhost/LAN):\n```bash\n# CLI flag\ntask-board --insecure q --format compact 'summary()'\n\n# Or environment (inherited by spawned agents)\nexport TASK_BOARD_TLS_NO_VERIFY=1\n\n# Or unified config file (project-local)\n# ./task-board.config.json  (NOTE: no token here — use 'task-board login')\n{\n  \"mode\":\"remote\",\n  \"local\":{\"board_dir\":\".task-board\"},\n  \"remote\":{\n    \"url\":\"https://board.local\",\n    \"board\":\"my-project\",\n    \"tls_no_verify\":true,\n    \"spawn\":{\"enabled\":true,\"max_parallel\":20}\n  }\n}\n```\n\n#### User management (service mode)\n\n```bash\n# Create user + token\ntask-board admin user create alice\ntask-board admin token create alice\n\n# Grant board access\ntask-board admin access grant alice my-project editor\n\n# List users\ntask-board admin user list\n```\n\nRoles: `admin` (full access), `editor` (read/write board), `viewer` (read-only).\n\n### Makefile reference\n\nThese targets are build helpers. They are not a replacement for `./scripts/setup.sh` when you need the installed skill copy, role sync, or self-update metadata.\n\n```bash\nmake                    # install-frontend (CLI + TUI)\nmake install-frontend   # CLI + TUI — developer workstation\nmake install-backend    # CLI + server — deployment\nmake install            # all three binaries\nmake test               # run all tests\nmake snapshot           # goreleaser snapshot (cross-platform build)\nmake clean              # remove binaries + dist/\n```\n\n---\n---\n\n# Part 2: Planning\n\nPlan command reference, phase planning workflow, `.planning/` storage, visualization — see [`references/planning-workflow.md`](references/planning-workflow.md).\n\nCanonical planning API:\n\n```bash\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan()'                          # whole project (all epics)\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(EPIC-01, mode=children)'   # single node: epic/story planning model\ntask-board q --format compact 'plan(TASK-01, mode=related)'     # single node: task/bug + dependency closure\ntask-board q 'plan(scope=[EPIC-01, STORY-02, TASK-03], mode=children)'  # set of nodes: union over their subtrees\n```\n\nScope forms:\n\n- `plan()` — whole project (all epics)\n- `plan(ID)` or `plan(scope=ID)` — single element scope (epic/story → children, task/bug → related closure by default)\n- `plan(scope=[ID, ID, ...])` — a set of epic/story/task/bug IDs; waves and critical path are computed over the union of the per-ID scopes. Unknown IDs are rejected together in one error. When per-ID default modes differ, the reported mode is `mixed`; pass `mode=children|related` to force one.\n\nCompact planning shape (`--format compact` + `plan(...)`) replaces the nested phase/element graph with an agent-friendly flat model — execution order without nested parsing:\n\n```\nactive:false\ncriticalPath:TASK-01 > TASK-02 > TASK-04\nelements:4\nmode:children\nscope:EPIC-01\nstatuses:{\"TASK-01\":\"done\",\"TASK-02\":\"development\",\"TASK-03\":\"backlog\",\"TASK-04\":\"backlog\"}\nwaves:[[\"TASK-01\",\"TASK-03\"],[\"TASK-02\"],[\"TASK-04\"]]\n```\n\n- `waves` — flat array of waves, each wave an ordered list of element IDs that can run in parallel\n- `criticalPath` — single-line ` > `-joined chain\n- `statuses` — minimal per-element fields (id → status only)\n\nThe default `--format json` plan output remains nested `scope`/`summary`/`phases`/`criticalPath`. Active out-of-scope blockers, dangling blocker IDs, and resolved elements with active blockers return a typed validation error that names the affected edge.\n\nLocal human-readable snapshot facade (presentation and persistence only):\n\n```bash\ntask-board plan                                    # full project plan\ntask-board plan EPIC-01                            # epic plan\ntask-board plan --save                             # save to file\n```\n\nAgents use `q plan()` above for phases/waves and the critical path; they never need to render a graph to plan. All render examples are centralized in [Human Visualization (Explicit Request Only)](references/planning-workflow.md#human-visualization-explicit-request-only) and apply only when a human explicitly requests a graph.\n\n---\n\n# Part 3: Agent Tracking\n\n## Assigning Agents\n\n```bash\n# Assign agent to element\ntask-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1)'\n\n# Remove assignment\ntask-board m 'unassign(STORY-260218-abc)'\n```\n\n## Agent Dashboard\n\n```bash\n# Show active agents\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents()'\n\n# Custom stale threshold (minutes)\ntask-board q --format compact 'agents(stale=60)'\n```\n\nDashboard response includes each agent's name, assigned element list (`id`, `name`, `status`), and `totalAssigned`, plus top-level `totalAgents`. The `stale` parameter filters old `done` items; it does not return timestamps or a child-progress counter.\n\n## Sub-Agent Workflow\n\n1. Coordinator breaks work into stories, assigns agents:\n   ```bash\n   task-board m 'assign(STORY-260218-abc, agent=agent-1); assign(STORY-260218-def, agent=agent-2)'\n   ```\n2. Each sub-agent works on its scope, updating task statuses\n3. Coordinator monitors: `task-board q --format compact 'agents()'`\n4. When all done — agents disappear from dashboard\n\n## Agent Roles\n\nRoles define what an agent does, which skills it needs, what statuses it drives, and its definition of done.\n\n### Role Registry\n\nRoles are defined in `.roles/<role-name>/` directories:\n\n```\n.roles/\n├── developer/\n│   ├── role.md        # frontmatter + markdown body\n│   └── skills.md      # optional: skill list\n├── researcher/\n│   ├── role.md\n│   └── skills.md\n├── tester/\n│   └── role.md\n└── ...\n```\n\n**role.md frontmatter format:**\n\n```yaml\n---\nname: developer\ndescription: Writes code, fixes bugs, writes tests\narchetype: implementer          # implementer | analyst | reviewer | tester\nstart_status: development       # FIRST child command sets this status after launch\nend_status: to-review           # role handoff status; for producers this means ready for review, not done\ndod:                            # definition of done — added to board checklist on spawn\n  - Code written per task description and AC\n  - text: Relevant tests written for new or changed behavior and passing\n    classes: [code]\n  - text: Relevant build/validation commands run after changes and build not broken\n    classes: [code]\nskills:                         # optional: inline skill list (merged with skills.md)\n  - go-tui-testing-tools\n---\n\n# developer\n\n## Description\nWrites code — features, bugfixes, refactoring. Writes tests for the code produced.\n\n## Standing Orders\n1. Add or update relevant tests for changed behavior.\n2. Run the relevant tests before handoff.\n3. Run the relevant build or validation command after changes.\n\n## Deliverable\nCode + tests.\n...\n```\n\nEvery board element has a `task_class`: `code` (the default), `metadata`, `docs`, or `research`. Set it with `create(..., task_class=metadata)` or `set_details(ID, task_class=metadata)`, and inspect it through the `task_class` q projection.\n\nRole `dod` entries may stay as plain strings, which apply to every task class, or use an object with `text` and `classes`. Spawn injects object entries only when the element's `task_class` appears in `classes`; an omitted or empty `classes` list preserves the legacy all-classes behavior.\n\n**skills.md format** (optional, merged with `skills:` in frontmatter):\n```markdown\n## Required\n- project-management\n- go-tui-testing-tools\n\n## Domain\n- core-data (when working on persistence)\n- swiftui (when building UI)\n```\n\n**Resolution order** (`LoadRole`):\n1. Project-local directory: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n2. Project-local single file: `<workDir>/.roles/<name>.md`\n3. Global directory: `~/.roles/<name>/role.md`\n4. Global single file: `~/.roles/<name>.md`\n\nDirectory format takes priority over single file. Project-local takes priority over global.\n\n**Global role installation:** `scripts/setup.sh` copies the bundled roles from `.roles/` into `~/.roles/`, making them available in any project without a local `.roles/` directory.\n\n**Available roles:**\n\n| Role | Archetype | Start → End Status |\n|------|-----------|-------------------|\n| `researcher` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `developer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `tester` | tester | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `solution-architect` | analyst | `analysis` → `to-review` |\n| `doc-writer` | implementer | `development` → `to-review` |\n| `reviewer` | reviewer | `reviewing` → verdict-driven (`done` / `to-dev` / `analysis` / `blocked`) |\n| `orchestrator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n| `coordinator` | analyst | orchestration-only, no default status transition |\n\nThe reviewer role intentionally has no unconditional `end_status`. Its role body selects exactly one verdict branch, so the default spawn template cannot overwrite a requested-changes or stop-the-line verdict with `done`. Only an accepted review sets `done`.\n\n### How Roles Work with Spawn\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are REQUIRED; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** Spawning without a role produces a crippled agent with no identity, skills, DoD, or lifecycle commands. Model and reasoning are deliberate caller choices and never inherit task-board defaults. `--agent` remains optional: pass a human/task-context provider only when the effective `preferred_agentic_system` allows it; omission uses the exclusive pin, an in-set mixed affinity, legacy preference, or plain affinity as applicable.\n\nThe spawn pipeline:\n1. Loads role config from `.roles/<role>/role.md`\n2. Loads task metadata (README + precondition resources)\n3. Computes pre-launch board updates:\n   - Sets agent name with archetype prefix, e.g. `[implementer] developer (codex)`\n   - Adds DoD items as checklist entries on the board element\n4. Assembles prompt from template: role body, skills, DoD, status commands, task metadata\n5. Persists prompt and spawn-run state under `.temp/prompts/` and `.temp/spawn-runs/RUN-.../`\n6. Returns the `RUN-...` handle immediately\n7. Background runner claims a queue slot under `spawn.max_parallel`, launches the real child agent, streams logwork, and executes completion hooks\n8. Agent gets FIRST command (set `start_status` after launch) and LAST role-handoff command (set end status) in prompt\n\n```bash\n# Spawn researcher for a research task (status → analysis)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-abc --role researcher --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn developer for implementation (status → development, DoD → checklist)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-def --role developer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Agent type follows the active schedule window, canonical provider system, legacy preference, then runtime affinity; model/reasoning remain explicit\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-xyz --role developer --background --model gpt-5.6-terra --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn reviewer (status → reviewing)\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-ghi --role reviewer --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n\n# Spawn orchestrator/coordinator for decomposition / orchestration / verification\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role orchestrator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\ntask-board spawn TASK-260218-jkl --role coordinator --background --agent codex --model gpt-5.6-sol --reasoning-effort max\n```\n\n`orchestrator` / `coordinator` is the special supervisor role: it decomposes settled specs into board elements inline, creates or refines tasks, spawns specialists, guards architecture, and owns producer -> reviewer -> rework -> reviewer cycles until accepted `done`. It must not create a board-layout meta-task, stop at `to-review`, ask the human to perform routine review, or do specialist implementation directly when delegation is appropriate. Spawn `solution-architect` only for genuine architecture decisions, not to perform the board layout.\n\nFor coordinator orchestration:\n- fan out children with explicit role/model selections; inspect `project_config().spawn.preferred_agentic_system`, omit `--agent` for exclusive/in-set-affinity resolution, and pass it only for an allowed human/task-context provider\n- keep the returned `RUN-...` ids\n- use `task-board spawn status/wait` as explicit synchronization points\n- sequence dependent work: wait for producer runs, verify outcome resources, then spawn reviewer/consumer runs\n- on requested changes, preserve the verdict, spawn focused rework, and send the next handoff through another reviewer until accepted `done`\n- if a child exits before handoff or parks in an intermediate status, inspect its run/outcomes and retry or reroute through a focused child; do not hand recoverable work to the human\n- use `blocked` only for a concrete external blocker or unresolved human-only platform/product/architecture/tradeoff/approval decision, after recording evidence, failed assumptions/attempts, viable alternatives and tradeoffs, the recommendation, and the exact decision or external input needed\n- do not shell-background `task-board spawn`; that duplicates the async layer and makes completion handling worse\n\n### Skill Injection\n\nSkills are injected as references (paths), not inlined into prompts. Claude, Codex, and Qwen use lazy body loading — only skill metadata (name + description) loads at startup (~50-200 tokens per skill). Treat spawned-role skill lists as lazy catalogs: full SKILL.md bodies load only when relevant to the concrete task scope, unless the user, task, or project explicitly requires one.\n\nThis means one `developer` role can reference many skills (swiftui, core-data, go-testing-tools, etc.) without context bloat — the agent picks what's needed for the specific task. See [`references/context-efficiency.md`](references/context-efficiency.md) for the selection rule.\n\n## Sub-Agent Prompt Templates\n\nFor sub-agent prompt templates (implementation agent and coordinator review workflow), see [`references/sub-agent-templates.md`](references/sub-agent-templates.md). Its legacy decomposition-agent template must not be used for spec-to-board layout. A `solution-architect` child produces only a genuine architecture decision; the Orchestrator applies that decision to the board inline.\n\nKey points covered there:\n- Reality check: how to maximize sub-agent compliance with task-board updates\n- Do not use the legacy decomposition-agent template for board layout; delegate only the architecture-decision deliverable that informs later inline board refinement\n- Implementation agent template (for execution phase)\n- Coordinator review workflow (approve / return for rework)\n\n## Spawning Tracked Background Runs\n\n**Tracked background children are separate CLI processes** (`claude -p`, `codex exec`, or `qwen` stream-JSON), each with its own context window. They are not internal sub-agents. Use explicit `task-board spawn` model selection to launch with automatic board integration and `RUN-...` tracking.\n\n**`--role`, `--background`, and `--model` are required; Codex and Qwen also require `--reasoning-effort`.** The role drives identity, skills, status lifecycle, and definition of done. See [`.specs/spawn-model-selection.md`](.specs/spawn-model-selection.md) for the no-default invariant.\n\nAgent type is a separate precedence layer. Canonical `preferred_agentic_system` enforces an exclusive pin or mixed allow-set; explicit `--agent` wins only inside it, and mixed otherwise uses in-set runtime affinity. An active UTC schedule window replaces that base system. Without the canonical system or active window, a usable legacy `spawn.preferred_agent` is selected and otherwise runtime affinity applies (`codex` from Codex, `claude` from Claude). A legacy preferred provider without a configured ceiling or executable falls back to affinity and records the path. After provider choice, the matching per-agent/role ceiling resolves model and any Codex/Qwen effort; a ceiling never changes the provider.\n\nUse `task-board q 'project_config()'` as the preflight check before spawn or spawn-contract review. `spawn.enabled=false` is an absolute project gate; `model_criterion=equal` forces the configured per-agent model without ranking it and forbids selection assessments. `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` may override `model`, `model_criterion`, and `reasoning_effort` field by field for that role, with omitted fields inherited from the agent ceiling. `less_or_equal` and `greater_or_equal` compare provider-local `policyRank` values, require a deliberate one-line `--selection-rationale`, record structural `(pair, text)` history per task and role, and require exact content-bound `--ack-resolved` plus whitespace-normalized fresh rationale text after a requested-to-resolved change. Treat [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md) as the canonical operator-facing contract. The durable wait/completion invariant lives in [`.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md`](.specs/spawn-runtime-completion.md).\n\n`project_config().codex.session.auto_continue` surfaces the effective Codex anti-stall defaults for this cwd. Those settings currently apply only to the optional `task-board codex` session enhancement; they do not restrict goal-bound Codex spawn from an `agents-infra codex` primary session. Explicit `task-board codex --auto-continue*` flags override config when provided.\n\n**Template-based prompt composition is board-centric:** `spawn` composes the prompt from the role template plus board state:\n- task description comes from the element README\n- task-specific DoD comes from checklist items already present on the element\n- role baseline DoD is appended after task-specific DoD\n- precondition resources carry additional instructions and artifacts\n\nHidden legacy `--task-path` / `--dod-path` flags are compatibility-only appenders. They are not the normal workflow and should not be treated as required task inputs.\n\nIf the element has no task-specific DoD on the board, spawn should fail with a clear validation error instead of launching an underspecified agent.\n\nThe prompt also includes:\n1. Role config (identity, skills, DoD, status commands)\n2. Task README fields (title, scope, AC)\n3. Precondition resources attached to the task (loaded and injected into the prompt)\n4. A Go template (project-local `.agents/spawn-template.md` or embedded default)\n\n**Orchestrator monitoring:** A valid explicit spawn creates a tracked background runner, persists run state under `.temp/spawn-runs/`, and returns a `RUN-...` identifier immediately. The response includes coordinator-facing observation hints (text and `--json observation.*`) with a seeded resume cursor (`RUN-ID:0`) and watch/events/wait commands that preserve the active executable plus board context. `spawn events` replays durable events, `spawn watch` follows the stream, and `spawn wait` observes persisted terminal state. Watcher notifications do not inject text into the active orchestrator session unless a runtime-specific adapter exists. Codex sessions launched through optional `task-board codex` get terminal updates pushed into the active thread; this session UX does not govern goal-bound owner launch. Claude Code sessions get the same non-blocking effect by running `spawn wait RUN-...` as a harness background task that wakes the session on child completion. The background runner blocks on the real child, executes the completion hook, and only then marks the run terminal.\n\n**Project spawn policy:** local profiles use `task-board.config.json -> spawn`; remote profiles use `task-board.config.json -> remote.spawn` and fall back to root `spawn` when `remote.spawn` is missing or `null` for backward compatibility.\n`spawn.enabled` defaults to `true`; `false` rejects every spawn before board\nlookup, mutation, prompt/manifest creation, or runner registration.\n`spawn.launch_composition.enabled` defaults to `true`; explicit `false` is the\nonly opt-out. After provider selection, task-board invokes\n`agents-infra compose --agent codex|claude|qwen --project DIR --schema-version 1 --json`\nonce for a new spawn, validates the complete MCP-only contract and provider\nallowlist, and prepends the accepted prefix to ordinary and goal-bound child\nlaunches. Missing, pre-contract, unsupported, failing, or invalid infra is an\noptional-capability degradation: launch the unchanged bare child and persist a\nsanitized `disabled`, `empty`, `applied`, or `degraded_*` status. Do not retry\ncomposition in v1 and do not fail the spawn for this capability.\n\n**Project MCP inheritance is a soft `relux-agents-infra` dependency.** It\nrequires `agents-infra compose` to implement\n`agents-infra.child-launch-composition` schema version `1` and return a\nsuccessful (`status: ok`) JSON document. `scripts/setup.sh` probes that exact\nCodex-shaped command and warns—not fails—when the binary is absent or the\nresponse is incompatible. Board installation and bare child launch remain\nfully functional in that state; only MCP inheritance is skipped, with\nsanitized launch-composition evidence recorded for the child.\n\nThe private run manifest may carry the validated MCP argv snapshot for the\ndetached runner and its restart/reroute/recovery successors. Public JSON under\n`launchComposition`, task notes, state, and events must omit argv, URLs,\ncommands, command arguments, headers, stderr, and environment values. Preserve\nonly MCP server names and referenced environment-variable names; bearer values\nremain solely in the inherited child process environment. Never apply\nprimary-session model, reasoning, profile, approval, sandbox, service-tier,\nprompt, or goal settings from infra composition.\n`spawn.max_parallel` limits how many tracked background runners may actively\nexecute child-agent work at once in one local work directory; when omitted, the default limit is `20`. It is not a cross-client remote-board scheduler.\nCanonical `spawn.preferred_agentic_system` selects and constrains the provider\nbefore ceiling resolution: exclusive pins one; mixed accepts an explicit in-set\nprovider or in-set affinity. Without it, legacy usable `spawn.preferred_agent`\nand then runtime affinity apply. A provider ceiling gates only legacy preference\nusability; after selection, ceilings constrain rather than replace the provider.\nFor each provider, `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.model_criterion: equal` forces the\nconfigured registered model and records the exact-model adjustment without any\n`policyRank` comparison. `less_or_equal` clamps above the configured\nprovider-local rank and `greater_or_equal` raises below it; ordered selections\nuse the deliberate rationale and adjusted-resolution acknowledgement contract.\nOptional `spawn.ceilings.<agent>.roles.<role>` objects override the agent\nceiling field by field for that spawn role. `project_config()` reports the raw\nconfigured overrides and `spawn.config_key_prefix`, while spawn selection audits\nreport the merged effective ceiling and the role-level config key.\n\n**Codex anti-stall policy:** project-local defaults for interactive Codex sessions live in `task-board.config.json -> codex.session.auto_continue`. Use that section for team defaults, and override per launch with `task-board codex --auto-continue`, `--auto-continue-delay`, `--auto-continue-max-nudges`, or `--auto-continue-prompt`. This v1 scope is `task-board codex` only, not generic `spawn --agent codex`.\n\n**Observed load-smoke:** with `spawn.max_parallel = 20`, a local smoke run of `30` fake-codex tasks yielded `20 running / 10 queued` on the first sample, then `20 completed / 10 running / 0 queued`, then full completion with `0` failures. Treat this as the expected queue shape for small homogeneous tasks.\n\nFor spawn flags, audit hygiene, template customization, orchestrator patterns, failure modes, and manual launch fallback — see [`references/tracked-background-spawn.md`](references/tracked-background-spawn.md).\n\n---\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
